 <exercises xml:id="hw-cond-prob" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>


 
<exercise xml:id="ex-tetris-bag-probability">
    <title>Tetris Game Mechanics and Bag Sampling Systems</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In modern versions of the popular tile-matching block game Tetris, pieces appear in shapes labeled <m>I</m>, <m>J</m>, <m>L</m>, <m>O</m>, <m>S</m>, <m>T</m>, and <m>Z</m>. To ensure fair gameplay, the engine uses a <term>7-Bag Random Generator</term>. This algorithm places exactly one copy of each of the seven unique shapes into a virtual bag, shuffles them completely, and dispenses them one by one until the bag is empty, at which point a fresh bag of seven pieces is generated and shuffled.
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose a brand-new game has just started, meaning the first bag of seven pieces has just been shuffled.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            Because the game samples without replacement *inside* any given bag, the trials within that bag are dependent. Pay close attention to how many pieces have already been removed from the bag when evaluating your conditional pools.
        </p>
    </hint>

    <!-- Part 1: First Piece Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-tetris-first-piece">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the probability that the very first piece dispensed at the start of the game is the <m>I</m>-piece (the straight line block)? Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places.
            </p>
      <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.1429"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! At the absolute start of the game, the full bag contains 7 distinct, equally likely pieces. Since exactly 1 of those 7 pieces is an <m>I</m>-piece, the probability is a simple uniform ratio:
                <me>P(\text{First piece is } I) = \frac{1}{7} \approx 0.1429</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2: Conditional Probability Within the Bag -->
    <task xml:id="task-tetris-conditional-bag">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose the first three pieces dealt from the bag are observed to be a <m>J</m>, an <m>O</m>, and a <m>Z</m> shape. Given this specific information, what is the conditional probability that the fourth piece dealt will be the <m>I</m>-piece? Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places.
            </p>
      <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.2500"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct!  Because the game draws from a closed bag without replacement, the remaining composition of the bag changes dynamically:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>Initial Bag: <m>\{I, J, L, O, S, T, Z\}</m> (7 items)</p></li>
                    <li><p>Removed Pieces: <m>\{J, O, Z\}</m> (3 items)</p></li>
                    <li><p>Remaining Bag: <m>\{I, L, S, T\}</m> (4 items)</p></li>
                </ul>
                Since the <m>I</m>-piece is still waiting inside the remaining pool of 4 equally likely options, the updated conditional probability is:
                <me>P(\text{4th is } I \mid \text{First three are } J, O, Z) = \frac{1}{4} = 0.2500</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>

    </task>

    <!-- Part 3: Idealized Streak Comparison -->
    <task xml:id="task-tetris-streak-comparison">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A player claims that during a live game match, they observed a consecutive sequence of three identical <m>S</m>-shaped pieces in a row. 
            </p>

            <p>
                Which of the following statements provides the correct mathematical evaluation of the player's claim under the standard 7-Bag Generator system, along with the correct underlying sampling framework?
            </p>
        </statement>


        <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The claim is possible but highly unlikely, with a probability of <m>(\frac{1}{7})^3 \approx 0.0029</m>.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This calculation assumes that every piece dropped is completely independent of historical drops. Because the standard 7-Bag system utilizes dependent sampling without replacement inside each bag block, a streak of three identical pieces cannot happen at all.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The claim is completely impossible under the 7-Bag system because a single 7-piece bag contains exactly one <m>S</m>-piece, meaning the absolute maximum consecutive streak length for any identical shape is capped at exactly 1.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. While a single bag is capped at one copy, consecutive streaks can span across bag boundaries. If Bag 1 ends with an <m>S</m>-piece and Bag 2 immediately starts with an <m>S</m>-piece, a consecutive streak of 2 identical pieces is entirely possible.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>The claim is completely impossible under the 7-Bag system because the maximum consecutive streak of identical shapes is capped at 2 across bag boundaries. To make a streak of 3 possible, the engine would require a selection with replacement into the bag.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Because each 7-piece bag holds exactly one <m>S</m>-piece, a single bag block cannot generate a duplicate. The absolute longest streak occurs if an <m>S</m>-piece drops on the 7th slot of Bag 1 and the 1st slot of Bag 2, creating a hard mathematical cap of 2 consecutive identical pieces. To allow 3 or more identical pieces in a row, the engine would have to switch to pure independent random sampling (sampling with replacement), where every drop maintains a flat, unconditional <m>\frac{1}{7}</m> chance.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The claim is possible if the system experiences severe memory lag, which dynamically stretches the piece distribution rate past the hard 2-piece constraint limit.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. Hardware lag does not alter the logical algorithmic execution code or mathematical constraints of the discrete bag array distribution sequence.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The claim is possible because if the player performs a specific layout configuration, the engine will force consecutive identical pieces using an alternative sampling framework to balance the sequence.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. The 7-Bag generator shuffles the piece sequence completely independent of a player's active grid layout. </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>

    </task>
</exercise>

      <exercise label="CP-2dice">
          <title>Roll Two Dice</title>
          <introduction>
              <p>
                You roll two fair dice. The sample space for this experiment is shown below.
                 
             </p>
             <image source="Images/Roll-Two-Dice.png" width="60%"/>
          </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the probability that at least one of two dice lands on the number 4, <em>given</em> that the sum of the two dice is 9? 
                    </p>
                </statement>
            
          <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
              <choice correct="yes">
                  <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.500 </m>
                      </p>
                   </statement>

                  <feedback>
                      <p>
                          Correct! 
                      </p>
                 </feedback>
             </choice>

             <choice>
                 <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.056 </m>
                       </p>
                 </statement>

                 <feedback>
                      <p> </p>
                 </feedback>
              </choice>

             <choice>
                 <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.111 </m>
                       </p>
                 </statement>

                 <feedback>
                      <p> </p>
                 </feedback>
              </choice>

             <choice>
                 <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.306 </m>
                       </p>
                 </statement>

                 <feedback>
                      <p> </p>
                 </feedback>
              </choice>

              <choice>
                 <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.944 </m>
                       </p>
                 </statement>

                 <feedback>
                      <p> </p>
                 </feedback>
              </choice>
          </choices>

        </task>

        <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                   What is the probability that the sum of the two dice is 7, <em>given</em> that at least one of two dice lands on the number 2? Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.182"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! 
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>

                  <test>
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.01"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Your answer is within 0.01 of the value wanted.
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>
                </evaluate>

            </evaluation>
            </task>
      </exercise>





<exercise label="ex-joint-probability-campus-coffee" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Probability Theory: Joint Tables and Independence Metrics</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A university campus coffee shop conducts a sustainability audit on a busy morning. The cross-tabulated joint probabilities associated with a customer's order temperature and their beverage container choice are detailed in the matrix below:
        </p>
        <p>
            <table>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell>Iced Drink</cell>
                        <cell>Hot Drink</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell header="yes">Reusable Mug</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.02</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.08</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell header="yes">Single-Use Cup</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.18</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.72</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose one customer transaction is selected completely at random from this audit group. Complete the interactive tasks below to analyze the dataset.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Marginal Iced Drink -->
    <task xml:id="task-joint-iced">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the probability that the randomly selected customer ordered an iced drink?
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.20" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
            <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the marginal probability of an iced drink order, sum the joint probabilities down the entire "Iced Drink" column:
                <me>P(\text{Iced}) = 0.02 \text{ (Reusable Mug)} + 0.18 \text{ (Single-Use Cup)} = 0.20</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Marginal Reusable Mug -->
    <task xml:id="task-joint-reusable">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 What is the probability that the randomly selected customer brought a reusable mug?
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.10" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate><test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test></evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the marginal probability of a reusable mug container choice, sum the joint probabilities across the entire "Reusable Mug" row:
                <me>P(\text{Reusable Mug}) = 0.02 \text{ (Iced)} + 0.08 \text{ (Hot)} = 0.10</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Conditional Given Iced Drink -->
    <task xml:id="task-joint-cond-iced">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 What is the probability that the customer ordered an iced drink in a reusable mug?
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin answer="0.02" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate><test correct="yes"><numcmp use-answer="yes"/></test></evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>P(\text{Reusable Mug} \cap \text{Iced})0.02</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part D: Conditional Given Hot Drink -->
    <task xml:id="task-joint-cond-hot">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 What is the conditional probability that the customer used a reusable mug, given that they ordered a iced drink?
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin mode="string" answer="0.10" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate><test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test></evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Evaluate the conditional probability:
                <me>P(\text{Reusable Mug} \mid \text{Iced}) = \frac{P(\text{Reusable Mug} \cap \text{Iced})}{P(\text{Iced})} = \frac{0.02}{0.20} = 0.10</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part E: Evaluation of Independence (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-joint-coffee-independence">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Based on the calculations completed above, are the events <q>Ordering an Iced Drink</q> and <q>Using a Reusable Mug</q> statistically independent within this campus audit? Why or why not?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>Yes, because the conditional probability of using a reusable mug remains exactly 0.10 regardless of the beverage temperature choice.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Two events are statistically independent if the conditional probability of one event matches its baseline marginal probability: <m>P(\text{Reusable Mug} \mid \text{Iced}) = P(\text{Reusable Mug})</m>.</p>
                    <p>Let us check the baseline marginal rate found in Part B: <m>P(\text{Reusable Mug}) = 0.10</m>. Since <m>P(\text{Reusable Mug} \mid \text{Iced}) = 0.10</m> and <m>P(\text{Reusable Mug} \mid \text{Hot}) = 0.10</m>, knowing the beverage temperature provides absolutely zero predictive information about whether the customer brought a mug. They are perfectly independent.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>No, because the joint matrix intersection probability is <m>P(\text{Reusable Mug} \cap \text{Iced}) = 0.02</m>, which is strictly greater than zero.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. An intersection being greater than zero simply means the two events are not <em>mutually exclusive</em>. Mutual exclusivity and independence are entirely distinct properties.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>No, because the absolute proportion of hot drinks in reusable mugs (0.08) is higher than iced drinks in reusable mugs (0.02).</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. While the raw cell numbers differ, they must be scaled against their column totals. Because the total morning volume features far more hot drinks overall (80%) than iced drinks (20%), the proportion of container choice inside each sub-category is what determines independence parity.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>Yes, because the sum of all individual cells inside the joint probability grid adds up to exactly 1.00.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. Every valid joint probability table must sum to 1.00 by definition. This structural requirement has no bearing on whether the variables are independent or dependent.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>No, because customers are physically much more likely to want a straw or a lid layout configuration that depends entirely on ice levels.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. Do not answer probability modeling questions using outside physical intuition alone. You must audit independence strictly using the arithmetic proportions provided inside this specific dataset.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>





<exercise label="ex-conditional-venn-calculations" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Probability Theory: Conditional Calculations from a Venn Diagram</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Consider the following Venn diagram showing the probabilities of various events and their intersections. For example, <m>P(A\cap B \cap C^c)=0.10.</m> 
        </p>
        <p>
            <image xml:id="tikz-three-set-conditional-venn">
                <description>A 3-set Venn diagram showing regional probabilities for events A, B, and C.</description>
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
                        % Render solid white background rectangle for canvas spacing
                        \fill[white] (-2.2,-2.2) rectangle (3.4,2.5);
                        
                        % Define the baseline circular geometries
                        \def\circleA{(0,-.45) circle (1.3)}
                        \def\circleB{(1.2,-.45) circle (1.3)}
                        \def\circleC{(0.6,0.6) circle (1.3)}
                        
                        % Draw the structural outlines of the circles
                        \draw \circleA;
                        \draw \circleB;
                        \draw \circleC;
                        
                        % Set and event namespace labels
                        \node[left] at (-1.1,-1.25) {\large $A$};
                        \node[right] at (2.3,-1.25) {\large $B$};
                        \node[above] at (0.6,1.95) {\large $C$};
                        
                        % Precise geometric coordinate placement for intersection values
                        \node at (0.6,1.3) {0.05};   % Only C
                        \node at (-.2,0.4) {0.15};   % A and C alone
                        \node at (1.4,0.4) {0.20};   % B and C alone
                        \node at (0.6,-.05) {0.15};   % Triple intersection (A and B and C)
                        \node at (-0.6,-0.75) {0.05};  % Only A
                        \node at (0.6,-1.0) {0.10};   % A and B alone
                        \node at (1.8,-0.75) {0.15};   % Only B
                        
                        % Universal Sample Space bounding box frame
                        \draw (-2.2,-2.2) rectangle (3.4,2.5);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </p>

    </introduction>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Remember how we think about conditional probability! <m>P(A|B)</m> is the probability that <m>A</m> happens in the universe where <m>B</m> has already happened. So, it may be helpful to reframe some of the probabilities within your “new universe”.
        </p>
    </hint>

    <!-- 1. P(A | C) -->
    <task xml:id="task-calc-a-given-c">
        <statement>
            <p>
                
                Use the Venn diagram to compute each conditional probability specified below. Enter all answers as decimals rounded to three decimal places.
            
            </p>

            <p>
                <m>P(C \mid A)</m> = <fillin answer="0.667" width="8" name="ansC|A"/>
            </p>
            

            <p>
                <m>P(A \mid C)</m> = <fillin answer="0.545" width="8" name="ansA|C"/>
            </p>


             <p>
                <m>P(A \mid C^c)</m> = <fillin answer="0.333" width="8" name="ansA|Cc"/>
            </p>

             <p>
                <m>P(A^c \mid C)</m> = <fillin answer="0.455" width="8" name="ansAc|C"/>
            </p>

            <p>
                <m>P(C-A \mid C)</m> = <fillin answer="0.455" width="8" name="ansAc|C_v2"/>
            </p>

             <p>
                <m>P((A\cup B) \mid C)</m> = <fillin answer="0.909" width="8" name="ansAB|C"/>
            </p>

            <p>
                <m>P(A \mid (B\cup C))</m> = <fillin answer="0.5" width="8" name="ansA|BC"/>
            </p>

        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate submit="ansC|A">
            <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            </evaluate>

            <evaluate submit="ansA|C">
            <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            </evaluate>

            <evaluate submit="ansA|Cc">
            <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            </evaluate>

            <evaluate submit="ansAc|C">
            <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            </evaluate>

            <evaluate submit="ansAc|C_v2">
            <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            </evaluate>

            <evaluate submit="ansAB|C">
            <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            </evaluate>

            <evaluate submit="ansA|BC">
            <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
       
       <!--
        <solution>
            <p>
                The new universe is circle <m>C</m>. Summing all regions in <m>C</m>: 
                <me>P(C) = 0.05 + 0.15 + 0.15 + 0.20 = 0.55</me>
                The intersection <m>A \cap C</m> is the parts of <m>A</m> inside <m>C</m>: 
                <me>P(A \cap C) = 0.15 + 0.15 = 0.30</me>
                Evaluating the conditional formula:
                <me>P(A \mid C) = \frac{P(A \cap C)}{P(C)} = \frac{0.30}{0.55} = \frac{6}{11} \approx 0.545</me>
            </p>
        </solution>


        <solution>
            <p>
                Intersecting the event <m>A \cup (B \cap C^c)</m> with the condition universe <m>C</m> simplifies to:
                <me>(A \cap C) \cup ((B \cap C^c) \cap C) = (A \cap C) \cup \emptyset = A \cap C</me>
                Because <m>B \cap C^c</m> lives entirely outside <m>C</m>, it contributes zero weight within the <m>C</m> universe. Thus, this is equivalent to Part 1:
                <me>\frac{P(A \cap C)}{P(C)} = \frac{0.30}{0.55} \approx 0.545</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    
        <solution>
            <p>
                Using the conditional complement rule within the <m>C</m> universe space:
                <me>P(A^c \mid C) = 1 - P(A \mid C) = 1 - \frac{6}{11} = \frac{5}{11} \approx 0.455</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    
        <solution>
            <p>
                The set difference <m>C \setminus A</m> means the parts of <m>C</m> that are not in <m>A</m> (<m>C \cap A^c</m>). Summing these regions: 
                <me>P(C \cap A^c) = 0.05 \text{ (only C)} + 0.20 \text{ (B and C alone)} = 0.25</me>
                Evaluating over the <m>C</m> universe:
                <me>P(C \setminus A \mid C) = \frac{P(C \cap A^c)}{P(C)} = \frac{0.25}{0.55} = \frac{5}{11} \approx 0.455</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    
        <solution>
            <p>
                The new universe is circle <m>A</m>. Summing all regions in <m>A</m>:
                <me>P(A) = 0.05 + 0.15 + 0.15 + 0.10 = 0.45</me>
                The parts of <m>C</m> that overlap inside <m>A</m> are: <m>P(A \cap C) = 0.15 + 0.15 = 0.30</m>.
                <me>P(C \mid A) = \frac{P(A \cap C)}{P(A)} = \frac{0.30}{0.45} = \frac{2}{3} \approx 0.667</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
 
        <solution>
            <p>
                The universe is everything outside circle <m>C</m>:
                <me>P(C^c) = 1 - P(C) = 1 - 0.55 = 0.45</me>
                The parts of <m>A</m> that sit completely outside <m>C</m> are:
                <me>P(A \cap C^c) = 0.05 \text{ (only A)} + 0.10 \text{ (A and B alone)} = 0.15</me>
                Evaluating the conditional fraction:
                <me>P(A \mid C^c) = \frac{P(A \cap C^c)}{P(C^c)} = \frac{0.15}{0.45} = \frac{1}{3} \approx 0.333</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
   
        <solution>
            <p>
                The universe is the entire combined space of circle <m>B</m> and circle <m>C</m>:
                <me>P(B \cup C) = 0.05 + 0.15 + 0.15 + 0.20 + 0.10 + 0.15 = 0.80</me>
                The sections of <m>A</m> that sit inside this combined union space are:
                <me>P(A \cap (B \cup C)) = 0.15 + 0.15 + 0.10 = 0.40</me>
                Evaluating the fraction:
                <me>P(A \mid B \cup C) = \frac{0.40}{0.80} = 0.500</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
   
        <solution>
            <p>
                The universe is circle <m>C</m> (<m>P(C) = 0.55</m>). The sections of the union <m>A \cup B</m> that are contained inside <m>C</m> are:
</p>
    </solution> -->
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
            Events <m>A</m> and <m>C</m> are _________.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Positively Correlated</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! To determine the relationship, compare the conditional probability to the baseline probability. From previous parts, we know <m>P(A \mid C) \approx 0.545</m> and the baseline probability is <m>P(A) = 0.05 + 0.15 + 0.15 + 0.10 = 0.450</m>.</p>
                    <p>Because <m>P(A \mid C) \gt P(A)</m> (or equivalently, <m>P(A \cap C) = 0.30 \gt P(A) \cdot P(C) = 0.45 \cdot 0.55 = 0.2475</m>), the occurrence of event <m>C</m> increases the likelihood of event <m>A</m> occurring. Therefore, they are <strong>positively correlated</strong>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Negatively Correlated</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. If they were negatively correlated, the conditional probability <m>P(A \mid C)</m> would be strictly less than the baseline probability <m>P(A)</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Independent</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. For two events to be independent, the occurrence of one must not change the probability of the other, meaning <m>P(A \mid C) = P(A)</m>. Here, <m>0.545 \neq 0.450</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Not Enough Information</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. There is sufficient information since all region weights within the sample space boundaries are explicitly defined on the diagram, allowing you to compute and compare both <m>P(A \cap C)</m> and <m>P(A) \cdot P(C)</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-calc-correlation-independence-ab">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Events <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are _________.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Positively Correlated</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. If they were positively correlated, the actual intersection probability <m>P(A \cap B)</m> would be strictly greater than the expected product of their individual probabilities under independence.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Negatively Correlated</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! To determine the relationship, let us compute and compare their intersection probability to the expected probability under independence:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><m>P(A) = 0.05 + 0.15 + 0.15 + 0.10 = 0.45</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(B) = 0.10 + 0.15 + 0.20 + 0.15 = 0.60</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Actual intersection: <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.10 \text{ (A and B alone)} + 0.15 \text{ (triple)} = 0.25</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Expected under independence: <m>P(A) \cdot P(B) = 0.45 \cdot 0.60 = 0.27</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Because <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.25 \lt P(A) \cdot P(B) = 0.27</m> (or equivalently, <m>P(A \mid B) = \frac{0.25}{0.60} \approx 0.417 \lt P(A) = 0.450</m>), the occurrence of event <m>B</m> decreases the likelihood of event <m>A</m> occurring. Therefore, they are <strong>negatively correlated</strong>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Independent</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. For <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> to be independent, we would need <m>P(A \cap B) = P(A) \cdot P(B)</m>. Here, <m>0.25 \neq 0.27</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Not Enough Information</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. There is sufficient information since summing the regional values completely defines the probabilities of <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and their overlapping spaces.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
</choices>
</task>

            
    </exercise>










   <exercise xml:id="CP-generic-practice-A" >
