    <exercise xml:id="CP-independence" >
      <title>Independence</title>
      <introduction>
          <p>
             Suppose that <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> are three mutually independent events for which <m>P(A)=0.20</m>, <m>P(B)=0.56</m>, and <m>P(C)=0.37</m>. 
          </p>
      </introduction>
    
      <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                  Find <m>P(A|B)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.560"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! The probability of B does not depend on whether A has occurred.
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>             
              </evaluate>
            </evaluation>
      </task>

      <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                  Find <m>P(A \cap B)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.112"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! When events are independent you can multiply them together to find the probability of their intersection.
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>             
              </evaluate>
            </evaluation>
      </task>

      <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                  Find <m>P(A\cup B)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.648"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! Independent <m>\neq</m> Disjoint or Mutually Exclusive.
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>             
              </evaluate>
            </evaluation>
            </task>


             <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                  Find <m>P(B-A)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.448"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! <m>P(B-A)=P(B\cap A^c)=P(B)P(A^c)</m>
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>             
              </evaluate>
            </evaluation>
      </task>

                  <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                  Find <m>P(A\cup B \cup C)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.778"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! <m>P(A\cup B \cup C)=.....</m>
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>             
              </evaluate>
            </evaluation>
      </task>
      </exercise>








  </exercises>



  <exercises xml:id="hw-cond-prob-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>



<exercise xml:id="ex-ball-selection-probabilities-tasks">
    <title>Combinatorial Selection and Conditional Probabilities</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A box contains six colored spheres: one yellow (<m>Y</m>), two red (<m>R</m>), and three green (<m>G</m>). Two spheres are chosen randomly at the same time (without replacement). Define the following events:
            <ul>
                <li><p><m>A</m>: One of the chosen spheres is yellow.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>B</m>: At least one of the chosen spheres is red.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>C</m>: Both chosen spheres are green.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>D</m>: Both chosen spheres are of the same color.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            Start by calculating the total sample space using combinations: <m>\binom{6}{2} = 15</m> possible pairs. Break down the 15 outcomes by counting how many match each combination type (e.g., <m>YR</m>, <m>YG</m>, <m>RR</m>, <m>RG</m>, <m>GG</m>). Use those sub-counts to calculate the conditional intersections.
        </p>
    </hint>

    <!-- Part 1 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(B \mid A^c)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                By definition of conditional probability, <m>P(B \mid A^c) = \frac{P(B \cap A^c)}{P(A^c)}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The event <m>A^c</m> means no yellow sphere is selected, leaving <m>15 - \binom{5}{2} = 10</m> outcomes. The intersection <m>B \cap A^c</m> means selecting at least one red sphere and <term>no</term> yellow sphere, which covers the <m>RR</m> and <m>RG</m> categories (<m>1 + 6 = 7</m> outcomes). Therefore:
                <me>P(B \mid A^c) = \frac{7/15}{10/15} = \frac{7}{10} = 0.70</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(C \mid D)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                By definition of conditional probability, <m>P(C \mid D) = \frac{P(C \cap D)}{P(D)}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The event <m>D</m> (same color) includes <m>RR</m> (<m>1</m> way) and <m>GG</m> (<m>3</m> ways), yielding <m>4</m> total outcomes. The intersection <m>C \cap D</m> requires both spheres to be green and both to be the same color, which is just event <m>C</m> (<m>3</m> outcomes). Therefore:
                <me>P(C \mid D) = \frac{3/15}{4/15} = \frac{3}{4} = 0.75</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 3 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(A \cap D)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This intersection requires choosing exactly one yellow sphere, while simultaneously picking two spheres of the exact same color. Because there is only <m>1</m> yellow sphere available in the box, it is physically impossible to draw a matching pair of yellow spheres. Therefore, these two events are mutually exclusive:
                <me>P(A \cap D) = 0</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 4 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Are any of the four events independent of each other? Explain your reasoning.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Two events <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent if and only if <m>P(X \cap Y) = P(X) \times P(Y)</m>. Let us test the relationships:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>For <m>A</m> and <m>D</m>: <m>P(A \cap D) = 0</m>, but <m>P(A) \times P(D) = \frac{5}{15} \times \frac{4}{15} = \frac{4}{45} \neq 0</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For <m>C</m> and <m>D</m>: <m>P(C \cap D) = P(C) = 0.2</m>, but <m>P(C) \times P(D) = \frac{3}{15} \times \frac{4}{15} = \frac{4}{75} \neq 0.2</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>: <m>P(A \cap B) = \frac{2}{15} \approx 0.1333</m> (the <m>YR</m> pair), but <m>P(A) \times P(B) = \frac{5}{15} \times \frac{9}{15} = 0.20</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For <m>B</m> and <m>C</m>: Events <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> are mutually exclusive, so <m>P(B \cap C) = 0</m>, but <m>P(B) \times P(C) \neq 0</m>.</p></li>
                </ul>
                Consequently, <bold>no</bold>, none of the four events are independent of each other. Every pairing exhibits mathematical dependence due to drawing without replacement from a small finite pool.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-gallup-poll-probabilities">
    <title>Gallup Poll Political Affiliation Probabilities</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A September 2024 Gallup poll asked: <q>As of today, do you consider yourself a Republican, a Democrat or an independent?</q> Responses are shown below, separated by gender, for the 1,000 U.S. adult respondents.
        </p>
        <p>
            <table xml:id="table-gallup-data">
                <title>Political Affiliation Survey Data by Gender</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell>Republican (<m>R</m>)</cell>
                        <cell>Democrat (<m>D</m>)</cell>
                        <cell>Independent (<m>I</m>)</cell>
                        <cell>Total</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell header="yes">Male (<m>M</m>)</cell>
                        <cell>158</cell>
                        <cell>133</cell>
                        <cell>218</cell>
                        <cell><m>\mathbf{509}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell header="yes">Female (<m>F</m>)</cell>
                        <cell>130</cell>
                        <cell>183</cell>
                        <cell>175</cell>
                        <cell><m>\mathbf{488}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell header="yes">Non-Binary (<m>N</m>)</cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell><m>\mathbf{3}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell header="yes">Total</cell>
                        <cell><m>\mathbf{288}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\mathbf{317}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\mathbf{395}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\mathbf{1000}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            For simple or joint probabilities (Parts i and ii), use the grand total of 1,000 as your denominator. For conditional probabilities (Parts iii and iv), restrict your denominator strictly to the sub-total of the given condition row or column before pulling the numerator count.
        </p>
    </hint>

    <!-- Part A Block -->
    <task xml:id="task-gallup-part-a">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Based on this data, if a random U.S. adult is chosen from the survey participants, find the probability that the person is:
            </p>
        </introduction>
        
        <task xml:id="task-gallup-a-i">
            <statement>
                <p>i. Independent?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We check the column total for Independents and divide by the grand total:
                    <me>P(I) = \frac{395}{1000} = 0.395</me>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
        <task xml:id="task-gallup-a-ii">
            <statement>
                <p>ii. Female and Democrat?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We locate the cell intersection where the Female row meets the Democrat column and divide by the grand total:
                    <me>P(F \cap D) = \frac{183}{1000} = 0.183</me>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
        <task xml:id="task-gallup-a-iii">
            <statement>
                <p>iii. Democrat given that the person is male?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    This is a conditional probability, <m>P(D \mid M)</m>. We restrict our pool strictly to the Male row total (509) as the denominator, and select the Male Democrats (133) for the numerator:
                    <me>P(D \mid M) = \frac{133}{509} \approx 0.2613</me>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
        <task xml:id="task-gallup-a-iv">
            <statement>
                <p>iv. Male given that the person is an Independent?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    This is a conditional probability, <m>P(M \mid I)</m>. We restrict our pool strictly to the Independent column total (395) as the denominator, and select the Male Independents (218) for the numerator:
                    <me>P(M \mid I) = \frac{218}{395} \approx 0.5519</me>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B Block -->
    <task xml:id="task-gallup-part-b">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Are the events <term>Female</term> and <term>not-Republican</term> independent? Explain your answer based on the data.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Two events <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent if and only if <m>P(X \mid Y) = P(X)</m> or <m>P(X \cap Y) = P(X) \times P(Y)</m>. Let us define the events <m>F</m> (Female) and <m>R^c</m> (not-Republican):
                <ul>
                    <li><p>The probability that a respondent is Female is: <me>P(F) = \frac{488}{1000} = 0.488</me></p></li>
                    <li><p>The total number of respondents who are not-Republican is: <me>1000 - 288 = 712 \implies P(R^c) = \frac{712}{1000} = 0.712</me></p></li>
                    <li><p>The number of Females who are not-Republican is found by adding Female Democrats and Female Independents: <me>183 + 175 = 358 \implies P(F \cap R^c) = \frac{358}{1000} = 0.358</me></p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                Now we check the mathematical equality condition for independent events:
                <me>P(F) \times P(R^c) = 0.488 \times 0.712 = 0.347456</me>
                Comparing this product to our joint observation:
                <me>P(F \cap R^c) = 0.358 \neq 0.3475</me>
                Alternatively, we can evaluate using conditional rates: <m>P(F \mid R^c) = \frac{358}{712} \approx 0.5028</m>, which does not equal <m>P(F) = 0.488</m>. 
            </p>
            <p>
                Because these mathematical values do not balance, the survey attributes are conditionally linked. Consequently, the answer is <bold>No</bold>, the events are not independent.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-restaurant-credit-cards-noninteractive">
    <title>Credit Card Acceptance Probability</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In a large city, <m>37\%</m> of all restaurants accept both Mastercard and Visa credit cards, <m>50\%</m> accept Mastercard, and <m>60\%</m> accept Visa. A tourist visiting the city picks a restaurant at random to have lunch. We define the following events:
            <ul>
                <li><p><m>M</m>: The randomly chosen restaurant accepts Mastercard credit cards.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>V</m>: The randomly chosen restaurant accepts Visa credit cards.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            Recall that <m>P(M \cap V) = 0.37</m> represents the probability of accepting both cards. For the independence check, test if <m>P(M \cap V) = P(M) \times P(V)</m>. To find the probability that a restaurant accepts neither card, calculate the complement of the union: <m>1 - P(M \cup V)</m>.
        </p>
    </hint>

    <!-- Part A -->
    <task xml:id="task-cards-disjoint">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Are <m>M</m> and <m>V</m> disjoint? Explain your answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Two events are disjoint (mutually exclusive) if they cannot occur at the same time, meaning their intersection is empty: <m>P(M \cap V) = 0</m>. 
            </p>
            <p>
                According to the given data, <m>37\%</m> of restaurants accept both cards, meaning <m>P(M \cap V) = 0.37</m>. Because this probability is greater than zero, the events overlap. Consequently, the answer is <bold>No</bold>, <m>M</m> and <m>V</m> are not disjoint.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B -->
    <task xml:id="task-cards-independent">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Are <m>M</m> and <m>V</m> independent? Explain your answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Two events are independent if and only if the probability of their intersection equals the product of their individual probabilities:
                <me>P(M \cap V) = P(M) \times P(V)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                We are given <m>P(M) = 0.50</m> and <m>P(V) = 0.60</m>. Computing their product yields:
                <me>P(M) \times P(V) = 0.50 \times 0.60 = 0.30</me>
                Comparing this product to our given intersection probability:
                <me>P(M \cap V) = 0.37 \neq 0.30</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Because these mathematical values are not equal, accepting one type of card dynamically alters the likelihood of accepting the other. Consequently, the answer is <bold>No</bold>, <m>M</m> and <m>V</m> are not independent.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part C -->
    <task xml:id="task-cards-neither">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the probability that a given restaurant does not accept Mastercard or Visa credit cards?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We want to find the probability that a restaurant accepts neither card, which is represented by the complement of their union, <m>P((M \cup V)^c)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                1. First, find the probability that a restaurant accepts at least one of the cards using the <term>Inclusion-Exclusion Principle</term>:
                <me>P(M \cup V) = P(M) + P(V) - P(M \cap V)</me>
                <me>P(M \cup V) = 0.50 + 0.60 - 0.37 = 0.73</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                2. Use the <term>Complement Rule</term> to find the probability that a restaurant accepts neither card:
                <me>P((M \cup V)^c) = 1 - P(M \cup V) = 1 - 0.73 = 0.27</me>
                Thus, there is a <m>0.27</m> (or <m>27\%</m>) chance that a randomly chosen restaurant accepts neither card.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</exercise>







    <exercise label="ex-static-conditional-convention-merch">
        <title>Fandom Convention Demographics Analysis</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
        An organizer at a multi-genre pop culture convention conducts an entry poll of attendees. The survey data reveals that 28% of the attendees are wearing apparel from an Anime series. Among those wearing Anime apparel, exactly 25% are also wearing merchandise from a Sci-Fi media franchise. Additionally, it is recorded that 35% of all surveyed convention attendees are wearing merchandise from a Sci-Fi media franchise.
    </p>
    <p>
        Suppose an attendee is selected completely at random from this convention crowd. Complete the analytical steps below to map out the structural probability space.
    </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part A: Conditional Probability Calculation -->
        <task xml:id="task-static-merch-conditional">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     What is the conditional probability that the randomly selected attendee is wearing apparel from an Anime series, given that they are wearing merchandise from a Sci-Fi media franchise?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    First, use the multiplicative multiplication rule to calculate the joint intersection probability of an attendee wearing both items simultaneously (<m>P(\text{Anime} \cap \text{Sci-Fi})</m>):
                    <me>P(\text{Anime} \cap \text{Sci-Fi}) = P(\text{Anime}) \cdot P(\text{Sci-Fi} \mid \text{Anime}) = 0.28 \cdot 0.25 = 0.0700</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Now, evaluate the requested conditional probability by restricting the target space to the Sci-Fi attendee universe (<m>P(\text{Sci-Fi}) = 0.35</m>):
                    <me>P(\text{Anime} \mid \text{Sci-Fi}) = \frac{P(\text{Anime} \cap \text{Sci-Fi})}{P(\text{Sci-Fi})} = \frac{0.0700}{0.3500} = 0.2000</me>
                    The conditional probability is exactly <strong>0.2000</strong>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part B: Dependence and Correlation -->
        <task xml:id="task-static-merch-dependence">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Determine if the selection of Anime apparel and Sci-Fi media franchise merchandise are independent, positively correlated, or negatively correlated. Mathematically justify your reasoning.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    To establish the statistical relationship, compare the conditional probability found in Part A to the unconditional baseline probability:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Unconditional baseline probability of wearing Anime apparel: <m>P(\text{Anime}) = 0.2800</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Conditional probability of wearing Anime apparel given Sci-Fi gear: <m>P(\text{Anime} \mid \text{Sci-Fi}) = 0.2000</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Because <m>P(\text{Anime} \mid \text{Sci-Fi}) \neq P(\text{Anime})</m>, the two events are not independent. Furthermore, because <m>P(\text{Anime} \mid \text{Sci-Fi}) \lt P(\text{Anime})</m> (meaning the introduction of the condition reduces the likelihood weight), the events are <strong>negatively correlated</strong>. Alternatively, this is verified because the actual intersection (<m>0.0700</m>) is less than the expected product under independence (<m>0.28 \cdot 0.35 = 0.0980</m>).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part C: Probability of Neither Item -->
        <task xml:id="task-static-merch-neither">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     What is the probability that a randomly selected convention attendee is wearing <em>neither</em> Anime apparel nor Sci-Fi media franchise merchandise?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    To find the probability that an attendee wears neither item, we compute the complement of the union of both events: <m>P((\text{Anime} \cup \text{Sci-Fi})^c) = 1 - P(\text{Anime} \cup \text{Sci-Fi})</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    First, apply the general addition rule to calculate the probability that an attendee is wearing at least one of the two types of merchandise:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P(\text{Anime} \cup \text{Sci-Fi}) \amp = P(\text{Anime}) + P(\text{Sci-Fi}) - P(\text{Anime} \cap \text{Sci-Fi})</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 0.2800 + 0.3500 - 0.0700 = 0.5600</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Now, apply the complement rule to calculate the remaining area outside of both sets:
                    <me>P((\text{Anime} \cup \text{Sci-Fi})^c) = 1 - 0.5600 = 0.4400</me>
                    The probability that an attendee is wearing neither type of merchandise is exactly <strong>0.4400</strong>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

</exercises>

  </section>
